WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.879
Hi! I’m Sandra, from HEARa. 

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.211
Welcome to Day 7

3
00:00:06.211 --> 00:00:08.300
of our 10-day Lipreading Challenge,

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.180
designed to help you develop
and practice your lipreading skills!

5
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.080
Hearing loss can have a 
profound impact on your life. 

6
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.147
It touches every interaction.

7
00:00:19.147 --> 00:00:20.608
It can affect your career,

8
00:00:20.608 --> 00:00:21.926
your relationships,

9
00:00:21.926 --> 00:00:23.839
and your happiness.

10
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.080
Join me to explore tips and tricks
on this YouTube channel 

11
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.720
to bring confidence and clarity
to relationships in all areas of your life. 

12
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.120
Today is a two for one.
We're going to be learning 

13
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.475
about W and Q.

14
00:00:41.476 --> 00:00:45.118
Let's start with the W sound.

15
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.220
To make this sound,
your lips round and then open, 

16
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.320
and your jaw drops.
It's actually a two-movement sound.

17
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.620
Try saying a W with me,
as in WON’T. 

18
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.000
Round your lips,
open your mouth, 

19
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.060
and drop your jaw.
WON’T.

20
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.400
And now that you've learned the W,
you also know the Q! 

21
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.242
Because Q is K + W!

22
00:01:20.242 --> 00:01:25.162
What do you already know about the K sound?

23
00:01:25.163 --> 00:01:26.880
It's invisible.

24
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.640
So Q is not visible.
You only see the W. 

25
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.840
So add Q = K+ W
to your list of invisible sounds! 

26
00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.220
Watch me as I say
these two words silently: 

27
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.940
QUEST and WEST

28
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.266
They look exactly the same.

29
00:01:56.266 --> 00:02:00.822
Here's another pair of words that look the same:

30
00:02:00.822 --> 00:02:02.340
QUAIL and WHALE.

31
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.600
So, “I think I'll order quail from the menu.”
would look the same as 

32
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.239
“I think I'll order a whale from the menu.”

33
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.920
if you were a physician,
confusing QUEAZY and WHEEZY 

34
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.260
might make a difference to your diagnosis! 

35
00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.659
Sometimes the Q sound
is not spelled with a Q. 

36
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.920
What word looks like wire, 

37
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.940
that has a Q sound,
and it's not spelled with a Q?

38
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.200
Wire looks like choir. 

39
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.740
Now let's get some practice
lipreading W sounds in sentences. 

40
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.580
For the next 10 sentences,
I'll say the sentence silently, 

41
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.180
then out loud,
and silently again. 

42
00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.140
Pay close attention
to the sentences, 

43
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.760
because you're going
to see them again. 

44
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.080
There's some memory work
involved in today's challenge. 

45
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.720
It's another two for one:
lipreading plus memory skills.

46
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.320
The topic is worms. 

47
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.308
Are you ready?

48
00:03:19.308 --> 00:03:20.340
Number 1

49
00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.180
and the sentence is:
Have you seen worms on the  

50
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.500
sidewalk after the rain?
Notice that I had 

51
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.000
a questioning look on my face.
My eyes got a little bit wider, 

52
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.860
and my eyebrows raised a little bit.
Watch me again:

53
00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.000
Number 2

54
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.300
The sentence was:
Worms are wonderful—they work so hard! 

55
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.300
Here it is again:

56
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.340
Number 3

57
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.040
The sentence was:
Does a worm make you squirm? 

58
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:34.740
Here it is again:

59
00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.639
Number 4

60
00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:55.980
The sentence was:
You will see worms in wet soil.

61
00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:02.939
Number 5

62
00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:18.960
The sentence was:
Wet soil keeps a worm’s skin moist. 

63
00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.599
Here it is again:

64
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:29.879
Number 6

65
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.880
The sentence was:
Worms to breathe through their skin. 

66
00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.520
Here it is again:

67
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.495
The next sentence has two parts.

68
00:05:53.495 --> 00:05:53.995
Number 7

69
00:06:02.015 --> 00:06:09.127
The first part was:
When it is wet and rainy outside,

70
00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:15.840
Number 8

71
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.740
The sentence was:
worms wiggle onto sidewalks. 

72
00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.220
So now I'm going to do the whole sentence again:

73
00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.320
The sentence was: 

74
00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.840
Worms can eat up to half of 
their body weight per day!

75
00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:03.900
Here it is again:

76
00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.040
Number 10

77
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:29.460
The sentence was:
Worms bring water and air into the ground.

78
00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.099
Here it is again:

79
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:48.420
Okay, now I'm going to randomly pick
five of the sentences that we just worked on. 

80
00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:52.380
See if you can figure out which
ones I picked. 

81
00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.999
It's totally okay to guess.

82
00:07:55.999 --> 00:07:59.160
If you can recognize it, you're lip reading!

83
00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.020
The answers are written below.
Refer to them if you're not sure. 

84
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.126
Are you ready?

85
00:08:07.126 --> 00:08:08.400
Sentence A:

86
00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.795
If you're not sure, you can just rewind slightly.

87
00:08:19.795 --> 00:08:22.440
Sentence B:

88
00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.280
Sentence C:

89
00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:43.200
Sentence D:

90
00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.720
Here's the last one.
Sentence E:

91
00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:09.540
Probably one of the most popular
W words for people with hearing loss 

92
00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.899
is not worms, but “WHAT?”

93
00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.200
“PARDON?” is considered to 
be slightly more polite, 

94
00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:25.140
but they're both actually
non-specific requests for clarification. 

95
00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.400
So when you're asking someone,
“What did you say?” 

96
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:35.940
you can use a specific or
a non-specific request for clarification. 

97
00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:43.020
A non-specific request such as
“WHAT?” or “PARDON ME?” 

98
00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.600
or “WHAT DID YOU SAY?” 

99
00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:52.079
is actually not so helpful for the person
that you're speaking with. 

100
00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:56.640
It doesn't provide any information
about what part of the message 

101
00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.440
was not understood or
what they can do to make it easier for you. 

102
00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:08.160
So a non-specific request
does not do anything to resolve  

103
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.839
the communication issue.

104
00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.006
On the other hand, a specific request for clarification

105
00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:17.190
is considered to be more effective,

106
00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:21.447
because it gives the other 
person some information.

107
00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.560
Some examples are:
“I heard the first  

108
00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:29.820
part about worms being hard working, but 
I didn't get what you said after that.” 

109
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:38.040
Or, “Did you say that worms eat up to 
10 times their body weight per day?” 

110
00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:45.720
Another tip is to give the other person
some information about what would help 

111
00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:51.720
in terms of the way they are speaking to you.
Most people are more focused on 

112
00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:54.480
what they're saying
than how they're saying it. 

113
00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.920
Such as:
“Could you slow down?” 

114
00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:06.180
Notice how I said that slowly—maybe
the chameleon effect will work for me here! 

115
00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:13.920
Or, “I can't hear you when the tap is running.
Hang on for a second I'll turn the water off.” 

116
00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:18.240
Next time you can't hear
what someone is saying, 

117
00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:23.100
try making a specific request
for clarification and let me know 

118
00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:27.360
how that goes for you in the comments.
I would also love to hear 

119
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.018
how you're doing with the challenge.

120
00:11:30.018 --> 00:11:32.160
I hope you find this helpful.

121
00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.680
If you want to learn even more
because there's lots more I can teach you 

122
00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:43.500
about lip reading and communication tips,
I encourage you to sign up for my online classes. 

123
00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.300
The classes are standalone
so you can take them in any order. 

124
00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:52.200
And you can just take one,
or if you really want to improve 

125
00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.960
your communication skills,
sign up for the whole series! 

126
00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:00.960
Please Like, Subscribe, and especially,
share with others. 

127
00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.400
Thank you, thank you thank you!
